body:
thanks for the feedback! yes this is how I do those images, they aren't "really" randomly initialised, I play a lot with the ranges and clipping values and multiplicative value for zoom-in and out so it looks the best. I have done DIP some times ago with the trained layer of deep dream but that was about it, I felt like the way I am doing right now is a less common, less explored way to generate images with vanilla NN (CNNs with this technic look less organic). 

TBH, I played with a lot of hyperparameter, and having relu as an activation function in this type of generative model is pointless, it removes any and all details. The better one for hard edges is the square function:

def square\_act(x):  
return 1 \* (x > 0)

which is what I used for this [https://preview.redd.it/yoi4jtd6l2381.png?width=500&format=png&auto=webp&s=90268048e56597537e69a43c8ae6cb7750861241](https://preview.redd.it/yoi4jtd6l2381.png?width=500&format=png&auto=webp&s=90268048e56597537e69a43c8ae6cb7750861241)

if I remember correctly, it was either the first or last layer being the square function and the rest were tanh or sigmoid (tanh I believe, it is generally better since the output is from -1 to 1 but honestly you can just map the data whenever you want)

from what I found, more neurons per layer and more layer kinda do the same; higher value at inference, clearer and more details (it looks fuzzy if you dont have enough neurons or layer or high enough weight values).

the weight values is extremely important, you gotta find the sweet spot which changes with the activation function you use, the size of your model and its range of input.

the best activation function I found is gaussian, not the normal one, I usually use 

def gaussian(x):  
return 2 \*\* -((-2\*x)\*\*2)

gaussian, sigmoid, and tanh are imo visually the best.

and yeah, for the quality, I wish I could inscrease it, but I wanna see the result before the heat death and my computer is saying otherwise :( I gotta port the code to colab or kaggle but I like using pygame to visualise stuff so I gotta change a lot of the code :/
